# Commit finished units (and before next unit)

Do not leave your own finished edits uncommitted at the end of a task, and do not leave unrelated edits uncommitted across tasks. Commit the coherent, verified changes you made when finishing a unit — and before starting the next unit of work (or switching concerns) — so the history stays readable and clean.

## When to commit

Commit when:

- **You finished your own task/unit**: your changes are verified and ready — commit them before ending your response
- **Before starting the next unit**: when existing changes already stand alone in `git log` / `git blame`, for example:
  - A feature slice or bugfix is complete and consistent — commit it before starting the next slice
  - A refactor is finished — commit before starting behavioral changes
  - Config / docs / code for the same concern are ready together — commit before switching concerns
  - You are about to start a different concern in the same request — commit first
  - Before starting a new request’s edits, if there are already coherent, finished changes in the working tree from prior work, commit those first rather than mixing them into the new work

Skip committing only when the user said not to commit, when the change is still broken, half-wired, or would not build/test meaningfully alone, or when prior dirty changes in the working tree are unrelated/unverified (in which case leave them untouched in the working tree and commit only your own changes).

## How to commit

- Prefer small, focused commits (one concern each) over one large catch-all commit
- Message: short, why-focused (1–2 sentences); match the repo’s existing style
- Follow the usual git safety: no secrets, no force push, no config edits, no `--no-verify` unless the user asks, no interactive `-i` / `-p` git commands
- Stage only the changes that belong to your unit; leave unrelated pre-existing dirty files or hunks out (preserve them in the working tree)

## Relation to “ask before commit”

Committing your own finished, verified unit (or a finished logical unit before the next unit) does not require a separate “please commit” from the user. Still ask first if the user said not to commit, if the unit is ambiguous, or if the commit would include secrets / generated lock churn they may not want.
